// Platform: Microsoft Sentinel
// Note: EmailAttachmentInfo is a Defender XDR Advanced Hunting-only table — NOT
// exposed as a queryable Sentinel Log Analytics table, even with the Microsoft
// Defender XDR connector configured (see docs/reference/sentinel-vs-advanced-hunting.md,
// Section 2/3). DeviceFileEvents IS available in Sentinel via that connector, but
// the join side from EmailAttachmentInfo is not — this file is kept for structural
// parity with kql/xdr/email/ and will error with "table not found" in a real
// Sentinel workspace today.
// Email — attachment landed on device
// Joins a received attachment's SHA256 against DeviceFileEvents to see which devices
// actually received and stored the file — the file-artefact half of
// kql/sentinel/pivots/phishing-to-device-compromise.kql, useful on its own when you
// just need "who has this file" without the full execution timeline
// Ref: docs/scenarios/12-phishing-email/investigation.md, docs/scenarios/05-malicious-file/investigation.md

let lookback = 7d;
EmailAttachmentInfo
| where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
| join kind=inner (
    DeviceFileEvents
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
    | where ActionType == "FileCreated"
) on SHA256
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, FolderPath, SHA256,
    NetworkMessageId, InitiatingProcessFileName
| order by TimeGenerated desc
